ance that this fall  Apple will quietly update the 16 inch  MacBook Pro with a 10th gen minor  updates like the possibility of Wi-Fi 6  but don't expect any major design  changes now as far as the lower end Macs  like the Mac Mini the MacBook Air and  the 13-inch MacBook Pro I don't expect  those machines to get refreshed with  intel chips anymore I'm almost 100%  positive that the next models will get  Apple silicon the only mac left is the  Mac Pro and what I think Apple is gonna  do is release an Intel based Mac Pro  right before the two-year transition  period is over now moving on to the  release timeline of Apple silicon Mac's  the first one that we should expect is  the fully redesigned 24 inch iMac and  rumors are pointing to the release date  being between October and the end of the  year I personally think that Apple is  gonna host an online-only October event  where they'll release some Apple silicon  Macs and potentially other Apple  products like a new Apple TV 4k and an  iPad air for now alongside the 24 inch  iMac we should also expect a new Apple  silicon macbook most rumors are pointing  to it being a 13-inch MacBook Pro which  will most likely replace the current  $1300 base model now this makes sense  because it was the only 13-inch MacBook  that didn't get updated to an Intel 10th  gen processor sticking to an outdated  hñ chip and as far as the Apple silicon  MacBook Air the reputable analyst means  you quo thinks that it'll either come in  the fourth quarter of this year or the  first quarter of next year and either of  those dates would make sense so the  lower end Mac's which are aimed at the  casual market of users will be released  first giving Apple and developers more  time to optimize performance before the  higher-end Mac's get updated so let's  get right into those it's now pretty  clear that the long-awaited 14 inch  MacBook Pro redesign is gonna pack Apple  silicon and I think it's going to be the  $1,800 model exclusively and I think  it's gonna be released alongside the 16  inch macbook pro also with apple silicon  and they'll both probably come with new  mini LED displays as far as the release  date rumors are pointing to them getting  released anywhere from june 2021 to the  end of september but it may even be  released at Apple's 2021 October event  now as far as the Mac  Meeny it's in a pretty weird spot since  it's on the cheaper side but it's also  used by professionals and then there's  also the fact that Apple used it for  their developer transition kids but  since we haven't heard any rumors about  the Apple silicon version coming anytime  soon  I'd place my bets on it getting released  sometime late next year since that's  when the last three Mac Mini models were  released now as far as the higher-end 5k  iMac there's a chance that Apple might  not even update it with an ARM based  chip until 2022 past the two-year  transition period since it's used by a  lot of high-end professionals but on the  other hand it would definitely be weird  for the 24 inch iMac to be using Apple  silicon for two full years while the 27  inch is still on Intel so for that  reason I think we'll see a full redesign  with a mini LED display and ARM based  chip in the spring or summer of next  year and then there's the iMac Pro and I  personally think Apple could potentially  wait until 2023 to finally update it  again with Apple silicon since they need  the extra time to get it optimized  before bringing it to the high-end  market and the only Mac left is the Mac  Pro and since that Mac is meant to be  upgradable I think Apple won't update it  with an ARM based chip until at least  2020 3 or 2024 and at that point they'll  either come out with their own Apple  dedicated GPUs or they'll find some sort  of way to have ARM based add-in cards  that are replaceable one of our viewers  mentioned something about CCI X allowing  you to interconnect the main central  chip and various dedicated accelerator  cards without losing much bandwidth now  I don't really know much about this but  it would totally make sense for Apple to  use this in their arm powered Mac Pro so  there you guys have it that's how I  think Apple will handle their transition  to their Apple silicon if this video is  helpful be sure to tap the like button  below and click that circle above to  subscribe thanks for watching and we'll  see you in the next video  [Music]

